@page "/components/accentcolorswitcher"

<PageOutlet Url="components/accentcolorswitcher"
            Title="AccentColorSwitcher"
            Description="BitAccentColorSwitcher renders a row of accent color swatches that re-theme the whole app live from a single brand color, can persist the choice, and can keep the first paint correct even behind a CDN cache." />

<DemoPage Name="AccentColorSwitcher"
          SecondaryNames="@(["Accent", "Brand", "Theming"])"
          Description="BitAccentColorSwitcher renders a row of accent color swatches that re-theme the whole app live: one picked brand color is fed to BitThemeFactory as the seed of a complete palette, applied through BitThemeManager, and optionally persisted (localStorage and/or cookie, see Persistence) so it survives a refresh - with two opt-in first-paint strategies (beyond the default None, which simply re-applies the accent after hydration) that keep the accent correct even when the served HTML comes from a cache, before any Blazor runtime is up."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          GitHubExtrasUrl="AccentColorSwitcher/BitAccentColorSwitcher.razor.cs"
          GitHubDemoUrl="../Theming/AccentColorSwitcher/BitAccentColorSwitcherDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.Extras" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.Extras"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The accent is owned by the scoped BitAccentColorService (registered by AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices),
            so every switcher on this page shows and sets the same one color - including the one in this site's
            own header, which is this very component.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Pick a swatch to re-theme this whole website live; the first swatch is the packaged palette's own
                primary, i.e. "no override". Every switcher acts on the one shared BitAccentColorService, so a pick
                here behaves exactly like one in the site header - including persistence, which follows the app-wide
                configuration (see the Persistence section; a fresh app persists nothing until it enables it).
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Accents" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                Offer your own brand colors using the Accents parameter. Any valid hex works as a seed - the palette
                is derived from it on the fly. The Name shows as the swatch tooltip and accessible label.
                (Note: on this website only the six default accents take part in persistence, since they are what
                the app's first initialization registered for restore validation.)
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher Accents="customAccents" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Persistence" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                The Persistence parameter selects the stores the picked accent is persisted to: LocalStorage,
                Cookie, or both (All); the default None keeps the accent for the current session only.
                The cookie is the only store the server can read, so it is what lets SSR prerender the page
                with the visitor's accent - enable it whenever the server takes part in first paint.
                The persistence is app-wide state on the shared service, fixed by the first initialized
                switcher (this website enables All), so give every instance the same value.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher Persistence="BitAccentColorPersistence.All" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="FirstPaintStrategy" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                The FirstPaintStrategy parameter selects how the accent palette reaches the very first paint of a
                page load, before any Blazor runtime is up - which matters when the served HTML comes from a cache
                (e.g. a CDN) that cannot vary on the visitor's cookie:
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <b>None</b> (default): no first-paint machinery. The accent (if persisted - see the Persistence
                section above) is restored and applied after hydration only, so a server-rendered page paints the
                packaged palette first and flips once the client is up.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <b>StaticCss</b>: a prebuilt stylesheet carries every offered accent's palette, each scoped to
                the bit-accent attribute the inline head script sets pre-paint. Recommended for a fixed set of accents.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <b>StoredCss</b>: every accent change stores a snapshot of the generated palette CSS in localStorage,
                and the inline head script injects it pre-paint. No static stylesheet needed, so the accents do not
                have to be known up front.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                The CSS strategies work hand in hand with the Persistence parameter: pre-paint, the accent is
                re-resolved from the stores Persistence enables, so with the default Persistence None nothing is
                persisted, no inline head script is even emitted, and the first paint has nothing to restore -
                first paint simply won't work without a Persistence other than None. The StoredCss snapshot
                additionally lives in localStorage, so that strategy specifically needs the LocalStorage store enabled.
                Like the persistence, the strategy is app-wide state fixed by the first initialized switcher
                (this website uses StaticCss), so give every instance the same value.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher FirstPaintStrategy="BitAccentColorFirstPaintStrategy.StoredCss"
                                    Persistence="BitAccentColorPersistence.All" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="OnChange" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>Get notified when the accent changes:</div>
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher OnChange="color => changedAccentColor = color" />
            <br />
            <div>Changed accent color: <b>@(changedAccentColor ?? "-")</b></div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="First paint setup" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                With the default None strategy the switcher works with no further setup, but the accent is only
                applied after hydration - a server-rendered page paints the packaged palette first and flips once
                the client is up. Picking a CSS strategy closes that gap: drop the BitAccentColorHead component at
                the top of the host page's head (App.razor in a Blazor Web App) with the same FirstPaintStrategy
                and Persistence as your switchers, and it emits the inline script that re-resolves the accent
                from the enabled stores pre-paint on every
                load (this is what survives a CDN cache) plus the palette CSS the selected strategy needs. The
                Razor tab shows the host-page half; the C# tab shows the optional stylesheet endpoint the
                StaticCss strategy can link instead of inlining. The underlying BitAccentColorSsr helpers stay
                available for hosts that need finer control (CSP nonces, custom placement).
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" Id="example7">
            <div>Use Style, Class, Styles and Classes to customize the switcher:</div>
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher Style="padding:0.5rem;border:1px dashed gray;border-radius:0.5rem" />
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher Classes="@(new() { Swatch = "custom-swatch", ActiveSwatch = "custom-active-swatch" })" />
            <br />
            <BitAccentColorSwitcher Styles="@(new() { Root = "gap:1.5rem", Swatch = "border-radius:0.25rem" })" />
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
